  III. THE FLAT TRIPLES (f = infinity)
  ----------------------------------------

  (2,3,6): product = 36. Euclidean tiling by (pi/2, pi/3, pi/6) triangles.
  (2,4,4): product = 32. Euclidean tiling by (pi/2, pi/4, pi/4) triangles.
  (3,3,3): product = 27. Euclidean tiling by (pi/3, pi/3, pi/3) triangles.

  These are the THREE Euclidean triangle tilings:
  (2,3,6): 30-60-90 triangle. Product 36.
  (2,4,4): 45-45-90 triangle. Product 32.
  (3,3,3): 60-60-60 equilateral. Product 27.

  Products: 27, 32, 36.
  27 = 3^3. 32 = 2^5. 36 = 6^2 = (2*3)^2.


  IV. VARYING ONE ELEMENT: FIX a=2, b=3, VARY c
  ----------------------------------------

  The (2,3,c) family:
     c       deficit           f      type   product
  --------------------------------------------------
     2          -1/3       -3.00    SPHERE        12
     3          -1/6       -6.00    SPHERE        18
     4         -1/12      -12.00    SPHERE        24
     5         -1/30      -30.00    SPHERE        30 <-- icosahedral (LAST spherical)
     6             0         inf      FLAT        36 <-- FLAT
     7          1/42       42.00     HYPER        42 <-- Hurwitz (f=42, MAXIMUM hyper)
     8          1/24       24.00     HYPER        48 <-- f=24
     9          1/18       18.00     HYPER        54
    10          1/15       15.00     HYPER        60
    11          5/66       13.20     HYPER        66
    12          1/12       12.00     HYPER        72 <-- f=12
    13          7/78       11.14     HYPER        78
    14          2/21       10.50     HYPER        84
    15          1/10       10.00     HYPER        90
    16          5/48        9.60     HYPER        96
    17        11/102        9.27     HYPER       102
    18           1/9        9.00     HYPER       108
    19        13/114        8.77     HYPER       114
    20          7/60        8.57     HYPER       120
    21          5/42        8.40     HYPER       126
    22          4/33        8.25     HYPER       132
    23        17/138        8.12     HYPER       138
    24           1/8        8.00     HYPER       144

  As c increases past 7, f DECREASES: 42, 24, 18, 15, ...
  f(2,3,c) = 1/(1-1/2-1/3-1/c) = 1/(1/6 - 1/c) = 6c/(c-6).

  f = 6c/(c-6):
    c= 7: f = 6*7/(7-6) = 42.0
    c= 8: f = 6*8/(8-6) = 24.0
    c= 9: f = 6*9/(9-6) = 18.0
    c=10: f = 6*10/(10-6) = 15.0
    c=11: f = 6*11/(11-6) = 13.2
    c=12: f = 6*12/(12-6) = 12.0
    c=13: f = 6*13/(13-6) = 11.1
    c=14: f = 6*14/(14-6) = 10.5
    c=15: f = 6*15/(15-6) = 10.0
    c=16: f = 6*16/(16-6) = 9.6
    c=17: f = 6*17/(17-6) = 9.3
    c=18: f = 6*18/(18-6) = 9.0
    c=19: f = 6*19/(19-6) = 8.8
    c=20: f = 6*20/(20-6) = 8.6
    c=21: f = 6*21/(21-6) = 8.4
    c=22: f = 6*22/(22-6) = 8.2
    c=23: f = 6*23/(23-6) = 8.1
    c=24: f = 6*24/(24-6) = 8.0

  The MAXIMUM is at c=7: f=42.
  This is because 6c/(c-6) is DECREASING for c > 6.
  The FIRST integer past the flat boundary (c=7) gives the HIGHEST f.

  f(2,3,7) = 42 is the maximum because 7 is the SMALLEST integer
  that makes 1/2 + 1/3 + 1/c < 1.
  The smallest violation of the flat condition gives the largest constant.
  THE FIRST STEP INTO HYPERBOLICITY IS THE BIGGEST.


